workbook :integration_cli_strategy, type: :design

# Integration CLI strategy — official CLIs, auto-generated CLIs, and the long tail

Three workflow passes (122 agents total) over the **708 unique integration providers** (deduped from the
873 integration toolkits). The question evolved: *which have a CLI* -> *which no-CLI ones are worth our own*
-> *which of those can we AUTO-GENERATE from an OpenAPI spec*. The last is the real lever.

## The headline

- **708** providers total.
- **98** ship an official first-party CLI -> compile THEIRS to wasm.
- of the 610 without: **545** have a public API, **419** are realistically wrappable.
- **253** of those have an OpenAPI spec we can AUTO-GENERATE a CLI from (218 vendor-published, the rest community/apis.guru).
- **Net: 351 / 708 providers (~50%) are reachable with LOW effort** — compile-the-CLI or generate-from-spec.

## The automation thesis — one engine, N specs (answers 'could we automate CLIs?')

An OpenAPI spec already enumerates every operation (path, verb, params, body, auth). A CLI is a mechanical
projection of it — one subcommand per operation. So we do NOT hand-write 419 CLIs; we build **one generic
spec-driven CLI engine** and feed it specs. Onboarding a provider = adding a spec, not writing code.

**Why it is trivially wasm-safe:** the engine does ZERO I/O. It is a pure function —
`(spec, argv) -> a described HTTP request`, and `(response) -> rendered output`. The actual network call
goes through the Dock/WASI host (host_exec / a host fetch capability), never the guest. No sockets, no TLS,
no syscalls in the module: deterministic, sandboxable, tiny. The guest sees a real CLI; behind it is one
interpreter + a spec — exactly the emulation thesis.

**Language:** not literal hand-written WAT (you would hand-roll JSON parsing — a false economy). The real
minimization is the no-I/O pure-function shape, language-independent. Best fits, all in our compiler set:
**Zig** (freestanding wasm, no libc, tiny), **Rust `no_std`** (+ a small JSON parser), or **AssemblyScript**
(TS->wasm, dead simple — likely the 'assembly script' half-remembered). Pick Zig for the engine.

**Build order:** (1) the generic engine + the Dock fetch capability; (2) wire the 218 vendor-published specs
(free CLIs); (3) the 61 hot providers without a spec get a hand-authored thin spec; (4) backlog the rest.

## Tier 1 — official CLI (98) — compile theirs to wasm

